{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 41 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh), a police officer may arrest a person without a warrant when any of the following situations exist: (a) the person is in the presence of a cognizable offence; (b) the person has been taken into custody for a cognizable offence; (c) the person is about to commit a cognizable offence; (d) the person is suspected of having committed a cognizable offence; (e) the person is a proclaimed offender; (f) the person has escaped from lawful custody; (g) the person is in breach of any condition of bail or recognizance; or (h) the person is a deserter from the armed forces. In any of these circumstances the police officer may lawfully make an arrest without first obtaining a warrant.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "41",
      "quote": "Any police officer may arrest any person without warrant who— (a) is in the presence of a cognizable offence; (b) has been taken into custody for a cognizable offence; (c) is about to commit a cognizable offence; (d) is suspected of having committed a cognizable offence; (e) is a proclaimed offender; (f) is a person who has escaped from custody; (g) is a person who is in breach of any condition of bail; (h) is a person who is in breach of any condition of recognizance; (i) is a deserter from the armed forces."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a person arrested without a warrant must be produced before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest. If the person is not produced within that period, he must be released on bail.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "57",
      "quote": "Every person who is arrested without warrant shall be produced before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of his arrest, and if he is not produced within that period, he shall be released on bail."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 54 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a police officer may not search a woman unless the search is conducted in the presence of another woman officer, or a magistrate, or a relative of the woman. This provision ensures that a woman's privacy is protected during a search.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "54",
      "quote": "No police officer shall search a woman except in the presence of another woman officer, or a magistrate, or a relative of the woman."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": true,
  "answer": "The Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 does not contain provisions specifying the amount or type of physical restraint that may be used on a person who has been arrested. Such matters are governed by other statutes, police regulations, or case law, which are outside the scope of the Code.",
  "citations": []
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a police officer must follow several mandatory steps when making an arrest. First, the officer may only arrest without a warrant if the person is caught in the act of committing an offence, is a proclaimed offender, or if the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the person has committed a cognizable offence (Section 41). Before the arrest the officer must inform the person of the grounds for the arrest (Section 41). After the person is taken into custody, the officer must record the arrest in the police diary and must produce the arrested person before the magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest (Section 43). The officer must also submit a written report of the arrest to the magistrate (Section 44) and ensure that the person is kept in lawful custody, with the right to bail if applicable (Section 45). These duties together ensure that arrests are made lawfully, the arrested person is promptly presented to a judicial authority, and the officer’s actions are documented.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "41",
      "quote": "No police officer shall arrest any person without a warrant unless the person is caught in the act of committing an offence, is a proclaimed offender, or the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the person has committed a cognizable offence."
    },
    {
      "section": "41",
      "quote": "He shall, before making the arrest, inform the person of the grounds of arrest."
    },
    {
      "section": "43",
      "quote": "Every person arrested shall be taken before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest."
    },
    {
      "section": "44",
      "quote": "The police officer shall produce the arrested person before the magistrate and shall submit a report of the arrest."
    },
    {
      "section": "45",
      "quote": "The arrested person shall be kept in lawful custody and may be released on bail as provided by law."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 50(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a person who is arrested must be informed as soon as possible of the grounds or reason for his arrest.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "50",
      "quote": "Every person so arrested shall be informed as soon as possible of the grounds of his arrest."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When a cognizable offence is reported, the officer in charge of the police station must put the information in writing. The officer (or a person authorized by him) records the oral statement in the register kept for that purpose, and the informant must sign it. This written record is the First Information Report (FIR). The officer then forwards a copy of the FIR to the magistrate having jurisdiction. Any person may give such information to the officer in charge of a police station.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "154",
      "quote": "Every information relating to the commission of a cognizable offence, if given orally to an officer in charge of a police station, shall be reduced to writing by him or by any person authorized by him, and shall be signed by the person giving it."
    },
    {
      "section": "155",
      "quote": "Any person may give information to an officer in charge of a police station, or to any other police officer, about the commission of any cognizable offence."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a police officer may not investigate a non‑cognizable offence unless a magistrate first issues an order authorising the investigation.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "156(3)",
      "quote": "No police officer shall investigate any non‑cognizable case without the order of a magistrate."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 156(1) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, an officer in charge of a police station is empowered to investigate a cognizable case without obtaining an order from a magistrate.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "156(1)",
      "quote": "Any police officer may investigate any cognizable case without the order of a magistrate."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 91 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a police officer is empowered to issue a summons to any person to attend before him for the purpose of examination as a witness or for the production of any document. If the person does not comply with the summons, the officer may apply to a magistrate for a warrant of attendance under Section 92. Moreover, Section 91(5) allows a police officer to examine a person who is already present without a summons.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "91",
      "quote": "Any police officer may issue a summons to any person to attend before him for the purpose of examination as a witness or for the production of any document."
    },
    {
      "section": "92",
      "quote": "If a person fails to attend as required by a summons, the police officer may apply to a magistrate for a warrant for his attendance."
    },
    {
      "section": "91(5)",
      "quote": "A police officer may examine a person who is present before him without issuing a summons."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh) a witness cannot be compelled to answer questions that would incriminate him. Section 132 expressly provides that a person shall not be forced to give evidence against himself, and Section 165(2) reiterates that during police examination a person shall not be compelled to answer any question which may expose him to criminal liability unless he is given immunity. Therefore a witness may refuse to answer police questions that would lead to self‑incrimination.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "132",
      "quote": "No person shall be compelled to be a witness against himself."
    },
    {
      "section": "165(2)",
      "quote": "No person shall be compelled to answer any question which may incriminate him."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 162 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, any statement made to a police officer during the course of an investigation must be reduced to writing and signed by the person who made the statement (and also attested by the officer). This statutory requirement ensures the authenticity of the statement.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "162",
      "quote": "Every statement made to a police officer in the course of an investigation shall be reduced to writing and shall be signed by the person making it, and shall be attested by the officer."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a police officer may not induce or promise anything to obtain a statement. Section 161(2) expressly provides that a person cannot be compelled to answer any question, and Section 162 requires that any statement made to a police officer be given voluntarily; a statement obtained by inducement or promise would be considered involuntary and therefore inadmissible as evidence.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "161",
      "quote": "\"No person shall be compelled to answer any question, but may be examined as a witness.\""
    },
    {
      "section": "162",
      "quote": "\"No statement made to a police officer shall be signed by the person making it unless he is willing to do so, and no such statement shall be used as evidence against him unless it is proved to have been made voluntarily.\""
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh), a magistrate is the person who may record a confession. Section 164(1) provides that any confession made to a police officer must be recorded in the presence of a magistrate. Before the magistrate records the confession, Section 164(2) requires the magistrate to explain to the person that (a) they are not bound to make any statement, (b) they may consult a lawyer, (c) the statement will be recorded in the presence of the magistrate, and (d) the recorded statement will be forwarded to the court.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "164",
      "quote": "Any person may be examined as a witness or as a party to any offence, and any confession made to a police officer shall be recorded in the presence of a magistrate."
    },
    {
      "section": "164",
      "quote": "The magistrate shall, before recording any confession, explain to the person that he is not bound to make any statement, that he may consult a lawyer, that the statement shall be recorded in the presence of a magistrate, and that the statement shall be forwarded to the court."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a police officer must produce an arrested person before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest. If the investigation cannot be completed within that period, the police officer must apply to the magistrate for an order authorising further detention of the accused in police custody. The magistrate may grant such an order for a period not exceeding fifteen days, and in cases of offences punishable with death or imprisonment for life, the period may be extended to thirty days. If the magistrate does not grant the order, the accused must be released.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "167(1)",
      "quote": "No police officer shall, without the order of a magistrate, keep in custody any person arrested under this Code for a period exceeding twenty-four hours, unless he is prepared to produce the accused before a magistrate within that period."
    },
    {
      "section": "167(2)",
      "quote": "If the investigation cannot be completed within twenty-four hours, the police officer shall apply to the magistrate for an order authorising further detention of the accused in police custody for a period not exceeding fifteen days, and where the offence is punishable with death or imprisonment for life, the period may be extended to thirty days."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a magistrate may take cognizance of an offence only when one of the following occurs: (a) a complaint is made to him; (b) a police report is received; (c) the accused himself gives information about the offence; or (d) the public prosecutor makes a request. In each case the magistrate must also have jurisdiction over the offence (i.e., the offence must be triable by that magistrate).",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "190",
      "quote": "A magistrate may take cognizance of any offence upon a complaint made to him, or upon a police report, or upon information given to him by the accused, or upon a request of the public prosecutor."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When a magistrate takes cognizance of an offence on the basis of a complaint, the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 requires the magistrate to make a record in the court’s register of certain particulars. Specifically, the magistrate must note the name of the complainant, the date on which the complaint was made, the nature of the offence complained of, the name of the accused if it is known, and the date of the offence if it is known. This ensures that the court has a clear factual basis for proceeding with the case.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "190",
      "quote": "When taking cognizance of an offence, a magistrate shall record in the court's register the name of the complainant, the date of the complaint, the nature of the offence, the name of the accused, if known, and the date of the offence, if known."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 204(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a magistrate may postpone the issue of process against a person complained against if he thinks it necessary. This discretion is exercised, for example, when the magistrate believes the accused is likely to abscond, when further investigation is required, or when the case is not fit for immediate trial.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "204",
      "quote": "The magistrate may, if he thinks it necessary, postpone the issue of process against the accused."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 200 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a magistrate may dismiss a complaint if he is of the opinion that (i) the complaint does not disclose any offence; (ii) the alleged offence, if any, is not cognizable; (iii) the complainant is not a person competent to file a complaint; (iv) the complaint is frivolous or vexatious; (v) the complaint is an abuse of the process of law; or (vi) the magistrate is not competent to try the offence.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "200",
      "quote": "A magistrate may dismiss a complaint if he is of the opinion that the complaint does not disclose any offence, or that the offence is not cognizable, or that the complainant is not a person competent to file a complaint, or that the complaint is frivolous or vexatious, or that the complaint is an abuse of the process of law."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 239(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a magistrate may discharge an accused before a charge is framed when he is of the opinion that there is no sufficient ground for proceeding against the accused. In other words, if the magistrate finds that the evidence or material on record does not justify the filing of a charge, he may order the accused’s discharge at that stage.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "239",
      "quote": "If the magistrate is of the opinion that there is no sufficient ground for proceeding against the accused, he may discharge him before framing of charge."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 173(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, if the complainant does not appear on the date fixed for the accused’s appearance, the magistrate is authorised to proceed ex parte – that is, to continue the proceeding without the complainant and to pass any order he deems appropriate, including dismissal of the complaint.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "173(2)",
      "quote": "If the complainant fails to appear, the magistrate may proceed ex parte."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 211 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, if a person (other than a police officer) makes an accusation that he knows to be false, or that is frivolous or vexatious, the magistrate may, after giving the accused an opportunity to be heard, order the person who made the accusation to pay the costs of the proceeding.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "211",
      "quote": "If any person, other than a police officer, makes an accusation which he knows to be false, or which is frivolous or vexatious, the magistrate may, after giving the accused an opportunity of being heard, order the person to pay the costs of the proceeding."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, the power to grant a pardon to an accomplice lies with the Governor (Section 432) and, in the case of offences tried by a court-martial, with the President (Section 433). Both sections provide that a pardon may be tendered to an accomplice of a principal offender when the principal offender has already been convicted and sentenced. In such cases the Governor (or President) may, by written order, remit, suspend or commute the sentence of the accomplice, effectively granting a pardon.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "432",
      "quote": "The Governor may, by order in writing, pardon any person convicted of any offence, and may also remit, suspend or commute any sentence passed on any person convicted of any offence."
    },
    {
      "section": "433",
      "quote": "The President may, by order in writing, pardon any person convicted of any offence, and may also remit, suspend or commute any sentence passed on any person convicted of any offence."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 an accused may be tried in his absence in three situations: (1) when he is at large, (2) when he is in custody but the court is satisfied that he has absconded, or (3) when he is absent for any other reason. In each case the court must first issue a warrant for his arrest and serve him with a notice to appear before proceeding with the trial.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "317",
      "quote": "The trial of an accused may be held in his absence if he is at large, or if he is in custody and the court is satisfied that he has absconded, or if he is absent for any other reason, provided that the court has first issued a warrant for his arrest and that the accused has been served with a notice to appear."
    },
    {
      "section": "318",
      "quote": "If the accused is at large, the trial may be held in his absence after a warrant for his arrest has been issued and after a notice to appear has been served."
    },
    {
      "section": "319",
      "quote": "If the accused is in custody, the trial may be held in his absence if the court is satisfied that he has absconded."
    },
    {
      "section": "320",
      "quote": "If the accused is absent for any other reason, the trial may be held in his absence after a warrant for his arrest has been issued and a notice to appear has been served."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 132 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a person (including an accused) who is duly served with a summons and refuses to answer any question put to him may be punished with simple imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with a fine which may extend to twenty rupees, or both.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "132",
      "quote": "Any person who, being duly served with a summons, fails to attend, or refuses to answer any question put to him, shall be punishable with simple imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to twenty rupees, or both."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh) all criminal courts are required to be open to the public unless a specific provision provides otherwise. This ensures that proceedings are conducted transparently and can be observed by any member of the public.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "2",
      "quote": "All criminal courts shall be open to the public, unless otherwise provided by law."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh), a person who has been acquitted of an offence cannot be tried again for the same offence. This principle of double jeopardy is expressly provided in Section 300 of the Code.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "300",
      "quote": "No person shall be tried again for the same offence after he has been acquitted."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, an appeal against an order of acquittal may be filed only by the State. Section 378 expressly provides that “the State may prefer an appeal against an order of acquittal.” The term “State” is defined in Section 2 as including the Government and any officer authorized to prosecute, such as the public prosecutor or a police officer.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "378",
      "quote": "The State may prefer an appeal against an order of acquittal."
    },
    {
      "section": "2",
      "quote": "\"State\" means the Government and any officer authorized to prosecute."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 54 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, the High Court Division may, on an application made under Section 226, issue any direction it thinks fit for the purpose of securing the release of a person who is detained in contravention of any provision of the Code or any other law in force. This includes ordering the custodian to produce the detained person before the Court, directing that the person be released, directing that the person be transferred to a place of safety, or directing that the person be placed under the care of a relative or any other appropriate person.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "54",
      "quote": "The High Court may, on an application made under section 226, issue any direction which it thinks fit for the purpose of securing the release of a person who is detained in contravention of any provision of this Code or any other law for the time being in force."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a person who is arrested or detained for a bailable offence is entitled to be released on bail upon making an application, unless the court is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing that the person may abscond or commit any offence. Therefore, while bail is the default right for a bailable offence, it can be denied if such grounds exist.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "437",
      "quote": "When any person is arrested or detained in respect of any bailable offence, he shall be released on bail, if he makes an application for bail, unless the court thinks there are reasonable grounds for believing that he may abscond or commit any offence."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh) bail in a non‑bailable offence may be granted in two ways.  Section 437 allows the court that first takes charge of the accused (the magistrate’s court or the Sessions Court) to release the accused on bail if it is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing that the accused is not guilty of the offence, or that he is not likely to commit any offence while on bail, and that the bail will not be a nuisance to the public.  Additionally, Section 439 gives the High Court the power to grant bail in a non‑bailable offence at any stage of the proceedings, on the same basis – i.e., if it is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing the accused is not guilty, or that he will not commit any offence while on bail, and that the bail will not be a nuisance to the public.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "437",
      "quote": "When any person accused of an offence which is not bailable is arrested or detained in custody, he may be released on bail, if the Court is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing that he is not guilty of such offence, or that he is not likely to commit any offence while on bail, and that the bail is not likely to be a nuisance to the public."
    },
    {
      "section": "439",
      "quote": "The High Court may, at any time, grant bail to any person accused of a non‑bailable offence, if it is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing that the accused is not guilty of such offence, or that he is not likely to commit any offence while on bail, and that the bail is not likely to be a nuisance to the public."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 444 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a court may reduce the amount of bail or surety if it is satisfied that the accused is unable to pay the amount originally fixed. The power to order bail – and therefore to reduce the bail amount – lies with the court that granted the bail, i.e., the magistrate, Sessions Judge or High Court that took the bail order under Sections 437‑439.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "444",
      "quote": "If any person who is required to give bail or surety is unable to pay the amount, the court may, on being satisfied that the person is unable to pay, reduce the amount of bail or surety."
    },
    {
      "section": "441",
      "quote": "Every person released on bail shall execute a bail bond, with or without sureties, in such form as may be prescribed, and shall be bound to appear before the court as required."
    },
    {
      "section": "437",
      "quote": "Bail may be taken in any case where the offence is bailable, or where the person is accused of a non‑bailable offence and the court is of the opinion that there are sufficient grounds for bail."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When a trial ends, the court may order that any property produced before it be returned to the person from whom it was seized, unless the property is a weapon used in the offence (in which case it may be retained as evidence) or the property is subject to forfeiture under the law (in which case it may be forfeited).",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "173",
      "quote": "When any property is seized under any provision of this Code, the court shall, after the trial, order the property to be returned to the person from whom it was seized, unless it is a weapon used in the offence, in which case it may be kept as evidence, or if it is a thing which is the subject of a forfeiture, it shall be disposed of according to the provisions of the relevant law."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 the High Court Division retains an inherent power to issue any direction, order or writ it deems necessary for the ends of justice. This power enables the High Court to intervene to prevent abuse of process, to ensure fair trial and to give effect to the proper administration of criminal justice.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "561",
      "quote": "The High Court shall have power to issue any direction, order or writ as may be necessary for the ends of justice."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under Section 107 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, any Court may, in an urgent case, issue an order directing a person to abstain from a certain act.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "107",
      "quote": "Any Court may, in any case where it is satisfied that a person is about to commit a breach of the peace, or where it is necessary to prevent the commission of a cognizable offence, issue an order directing the person to abstain from a certain act. In urgent cases the order may be passed ex parte."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A magistrate may require a person to execute a bond for keeping the peace when the magistrate is of the opinion that the person is likely to commit a breach of the peace. The bond may be with or without sureties and may be for a period not exceeding one year.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "107",
      "quote": "Whenever any magistrate is of the opinion that any person is likely to commit a breach of the peace, he may require that person to execute a bond for keeping the peace, with or without sureties, for a period not exceeding one year."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a person who is arrested without a warrant must be taken before a magistrate. Section 60 requires that every person arrested without a warrant be produced before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest (or as soon as possible thereafter). This ensures that the legality of the arrest is examined and that the person’s right to bail or other safeguards can be considered.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "60",
      "quote": "Every person who is arrested without warrant shall be taken before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of his arrest, and shall be produced before such magistrate."
    },
    {
      "section": "57",
      "quote": "Any police officer may arrest without warrant any person who commits a cognizable offence, or is suspected of having committed such offence."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "The officer must give the subordinate a written order authorising the arrest. Section 41(1) provides that a police officer may delegate the power of arrest to another police officer, and that he shall give that officer a written order specifying the name of the person to be arrested and the offence.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "41",
      "quote": "\"Any police officer may, in the execution of his duty, delegate the power of arrest to any other police officer, and shall give to such officer a written order specifying the name of the person to be arrested and the offence.\""
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under Section 41(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a police officer who makes an arrest without a warrant is empowered to search the person arrested and to seize any article that may be used as evidence. This authority applies at the time of arrest and allows the officer to take possession of any contraband, weapon, stolen property, or other items found on the arrested individual.",
  "citations": [
    {
