  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 an accused who absconds is dealt with primarily under Sections 207, 208, 209, 210 and 211. Section 207 provides that if the accused absconds after the trial has commenced, the court may issue a warrant for his arrest and may continue the trial in his absence. Section 208 allows the court to proceed with the trial even when the accused is absent, provided the accused has been given notice of the trial date. Section 209 deals with situations where the accused is in custody but has not been produced before the court; the trial may proceed in his absence. Sections 210 and 211 address bail‑related absences – if an accused released on bail fails to appear, the court may again issue a warrant for his arrest and may continue the proceedings without him. In all such cases the court may also order the forfeiture of bail and, where appropriate, attach the accused’s property under the relevant provisions.",
