  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a magistrate has several powers when a complaint appears to be false. Section 190 empowers the magistrate to dismiss a complaint if it is frivolous, vexatious or lacks sufficient ground. More specifically, Section 203 states that if the magistrate thinks the complaint is false (or the complainant is not a proper person to make it), he may dismiss the complaint outright. If the magistrate determines that a false complaint has been made knowingly, the complainant can be prosecuted under Section 211, which provides for punishment for making a false statement in a complaint. Additionally, before dismissing, the magistrate may order an investigation (Section 191) if a cognizable offence is disclosed, or a further inquiry (Section 192) if a non‑cognizable offence is disclosed, but where the complaint is found to be false the usual course is dismissal under Section 203.",
  "citations": [
