{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 (Bangladesh) a confession may be recorded only after the following safeguards are observed:\n\n1. **Before the confession is recorded** the magistrate must be satisfied that the confession is made voluntarily and is not the result of any inducement, threat, promise or fraud (Sec. 164). The magistrate may also order a medical examination of the accused to ascertain his physical and mental condition before the confession is taken (Sec. 165).\n2. **During the recording** the magistrate must:\n   - Have the accused make the confession in writing, or if the accused is unable to write, have it typed and signed by the magistrate and two witnesses (Sec. 164).\n   - Read the confession aloud to the accused and ask whether he understands its contents and whether he wishes to amend or withdraw it (Sec. 164).\n   - Ensure that the accused is aware of his right to legal counsel and that no coercion is being applied (Sec. 164).\n   - Sign and date the confession, and have it entered in the court‑record (Sec. 164).\n3. After the confession is recorded, the magistrate must keep the original document in the court’s register and provide a copy to the accused (Sec. 164).\n\nThese procedural requirements are intended to protect the voluntariness and reliability of any confession used in criminal proceedings.",
  "citations": [
