{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 a magistrate has several duties when a person is arrested and detained. First, the arrested person must be produced before a magistrate within twenty‑four hours of the arrest (excluding travel time). The magistrate must examine the person and the circumstances of the arrest to ensure it was lawful. If the police claim that further investigation is needed, the magistrate may order the accused to be remanded in custody for up to fifteen days (or longer with the court’s permission). The magistrate also decides whether the accused should be released on bail, taking into account the nature of the offence and the risk of the accused fleeing or tampering with evidence. If the magistrate is satisfied that the investigation cannot be completed within the prescribed period, he may order further detention or direct the police to file a charge‑sheet. In all cases the magistrate must keep a record of the production and any orders made.",
  "citations": [
